# The PAM stack, in /usr/lib/pam.d rather than /etc/pam.d.
#
# It exists because of a measured failure, not by anticipation. user@1000.service
# failed with `PAM:accounting acct="neutrinos" res=failed`, and the cause is
# structural: ADR-0004 delivers /etc exclusively by signed confext, PLN-0002-02
# carved 1352 entries out of /etc, and Fedora puts the entire PAM stack in
# /etc/pam.d. systemd ships /usr/lib/pam.d/systemd-user, but that file includes
# `system-auth`, which on Fedora comes from authselect under /etc and therefore
# does not exist here. Every PAM-mediated login fails, which means no user
# manager, which means no graphical session.
#
# /usr/lib/pam.d is where Linux-PAM looks when /etc/pam.d has no match, so this
# is the upstream-sanctioned location rather than a workaround. Authentication
# policy for a role is release content on the same argument as the compositor
# configuration beside it: it is not something an individual machine owns.
#
# FIXTURE-GRADE. This is the minimum that lets a session start in a disposable
# VM. It is not a reviewed authentication policy, it establishes no credential
# authority, and it must not reach a physical host without review -- lockout,
# account expiry, password quality, faillock and the whole delay policy are
# absent, deliberately, because guessing at them would look like a decision.

auth        required                                     pam_env.so
# nullok, and it is doing real work rather than being sloppy: the session
# account is created by systemd-sysusers with no password, because a password
# inside a release artifact would be a secret shipped to every machine. The
# harness supplies credentials. On a physical host this line is wrong and is the
# first thing a review must change.
auth        sufficient                                   pam_unix.so nullok
auth        required                                     pam_deny.so

account     required                                     pam_unix.so
account     required                                     pam_permit.so

password    required                                     pam_unix.so shadow nullok

session     required                                     pam_limits.so
session     required                                     pam_unix.so
# What creates XDG_RUNTIME_DIR and registers the session with logind. Without
# it there is no /run/user/1000, and a Wayland compositor has nowhere to put its
# socket.
session     optional                                     pam_systemd.so
